---
import CaseStudyLayout from "@layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyHero from "@components/CaseStudyHero.astro";
import CaseStudyMeta from "@components/CaseStudyMeta.astro";
import Figure from "@components/Figure.astro";
import ProcessColumns from "@components/ProcessColumns.astro";
import Pivot from "@components/Pivot.astro";
import Strip from "@components/Strip.astro";
import Metrics from "@components/Metrics.astro";
import NextCaseStudy from "@components/NextCaseStudy.astro";

import hivaz01 from "@img/hivaz-01.webp";
import hivaz02 from "@img/hivaz-02.webp";
import hivaz03 from "@img/hivaz-03.webp";
import hivaz04 from "@img/hivaz-04.webp";
import hivaz05 from "@img/hivaz-05.webp";
import hivaz06 from "@img/hivaz-06.webp";
import hivaz07 from "@img/hivaz-07.webp";
import hivaz08 from "@img/hivaz-08.webp";
import hivaz09 from "@img/hivaz-09.webp";
import hivaz10 from "@img/hivaz-10.webp";
import hivaz13 from "@img/hivaz-13.webp";

const title = "Designing HIV care for the people outreach was missing";
const description =
	"Arizona's HIV portal was losing the people its programs most needed to reach. A redesign built for them first, around a single question.";
---

<CaseStudyLayout title={title} description={description}>
	<Fragment slot="hero">
	<CaseStudyHero eyebrow={["UX design", "Capacity 4 Health", "Aunt Rita's Foundation"]} title="Designing HIV care for the people outreach was missing">
		Arizona's HIV portal was losing the people its programs most needed to reach. I chose to design for them first, cut a quiz that tested well down to a single question, and shipped a site that starts a conversation instead of a search.
	</CaseStudyHero>

	<CaseStudyMeta
		items={[
			{ label: "Role", value: "UX designer, solo" },
			{ label: "Partner", value: "Aunt Rita's Foundation, for Arizona DHS" },
			{ label: "Timeline", value: "3 months" },
			{ label: "Research base", value: "20+ counselor interviews, 5 lived-experience interviews, 1 co-design workshop" },
		]}
	/>
	</Fragment>

	<Figure
		src={hivaz01}
		alt="The shipped HIVAZ.org landing screen: 'OK. Let's start. Tap your status.' above three large buttons, a plus, a minus, and a question mark, on a purple background"
		figNum="Fig. 01"
		lead="A conversation, not a search."
		wide
		fit="contain"
		deck
		deckLabel="Shipped"
	>
		The shipped site asks one question before anything else, and the answer decides what comes next.
	</Figure>

	<section id="stakes">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2>A site losing the people it was for</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>HIVAZ.org is the state's front door to HIV care. Three people at Aunt Rita's Foundation run it, and none of them are developers. Most visitors arrived on a phone, and 80% of them left after one page. The design didn't help. "All the red and orange makes me think there's something urgent," one tester said. "I'm already stressed."</p>
		<Figure
			src={hivaz02}
			alt="The HIVAZ.org homepage before the redesign: a red and orange logo, an orange search button over a photo of Phoenix, then two full-width bands of icons and buttons, one orange and one red, and a red About section"
			figNum="Fig. 02"
			lead="Every page read as an emergency."
			tall
			deck
			deckLabel="Old site"
		>
			Red and orange throughout, including the pages with nothing urgent to say, for visitors who were already frightened.
		</Figure>
		<p>The state wanted more people using its HIV programs. But those programs don't measure success by traffic. <strong class="deck-stat" data-deck-foot="How HIV testing programs measure success|HIVAZ.org">A testing program succeeds when it reaches people who have HIV and don't know it yet, and they are the hardest people to reach.</strong></p>
	</section>

	<section id="audience">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2>Designing for the people programs most needed</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>Stigma came up in nearly every interview, usually before the website did. I've been through a diagnosis myself, and I worked to keep my story from standing in for anyone else's.</p>
		<Figure
			src={hivaz03}
			alt="Journey map with three bands: guiding principles across the top, an emotional experience curve and user flow through Discover, Compare, Select, Verify, and Follow-Through, and a row of opportunities below. The curve dips at the start, peaks while selecting a provider, and drops again in person."
			figNum="Fig. 03"
			lead="Two lows, and a false peak between them."
			wide
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Journey map"
		>
			Confidence drops at the start, "where do I even start with these search results?", climbs while choosing a provider, then falls again in person: "they told me they're not taking new clients."
		</Figure>
		<p>People with money and steady access to care usually already have prevention, so they rarely test positive. They were also the easiest people to design for, and existing outreach already served them well. The people the programs most needed had the least access: unhoused, immigrant, BIPOC, and underage Arizonans. "I need someone to be my lifeline," one person told me.</p>
		<Figure
			src={hivaz04}
			alt="A two-by-two: aware versus unaware of resources on the vertical axis, less versus more empowered to act on the horizontal. Four user archetypes with a quote and needs each: Meet me where I'm at, Self-sufficient, Needing a guide, and Activated."
			figNum="Fig. 04"
			lead="The people the programs most needed."
			tall
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Who we chose"
		>
			Unaware of the care available and least able to act on it, the "needing a guide" group was the least served by outreach. Their needs, guidance and positive feedback, became the brief.
		</Figure>
		<p><strong class="deck-stat" data-deck-foot="Who the redesign prioritized|HIVAZ.org">Health organizations know these are the people to reach. The difficulty is reaching them.</strong> So the question shifted from who the site was for to what someone frightened, with little support, could actually do on a phone. The answer cost the site its original promise. HIVAZ.org was built to be "all things HIV in Arizona," and designing for people with the least access meant moving most of that information into the background so the site could do one thing well.</p>
	</section>

	<section id="quiz">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2>Cutting a quiz that tested well</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>My first two concepts, a checklist and a provider directory, both added structure, and both overwhelmed people who were already anxious. The third, a short quiz ending in recommended providers, did remarkably well in testing. The question was who it did well with. Each added question helped people with resources, who wanted specifics about providers and the convenience of matching care to their own needs.</p>
		<ProcessColumns
			deckLabel="Three concepts"
			deckCaption="Each concept added structure, and the one that tested best served the people who needed help least."
			items={[
				{
					title: "An action plan",
					imgSrc: hivaz05,
					imgAlt:
						"Wireframe of the action-plan concept: two phone screens titled 'Your HIV action plan', with Step 1 Find a provider expanded into a checklist, then Step 2 Taking an HIV test expanded on the second screen",
					body: "A checklist of next steps. It kept people focused, but for someone already anxious it was one more thing to carry.",
				},
				{
					title: "A provider directory",
					imgSrc: hivaz06,
					imgAlt:
						"Wireframe of the directory concept: a city or zip search, then a Refine Search panel with type of service, availability, open hours, distance, and rows of speciality and population filter chips",
					body: "Yelp for HIV care. People wanted to choose a provider, but the gentle tone they needed disappeared into a grid of listings.",
				},
				{
					title: "A quiz",
					imgSrc: hivaz07,
					imgAlt:
						"Wireframe of the guided-search concept: 'Find the right HIV services', promising to identify the services you need, give you a short-list of providers, and provide a script for the phone call, with a Ready? button",
					body: "A few questions ending in recommended providers. It tested remarkably well, mostly with people who had resources and wanted specifics.",
				},
			]}
		/>
		<p style="margin-top:40px">That finding brought the tension with Aunt Rita's Foundation into the open. Giving those users the detail they wanted meant gathering more provider information than a team of three could handle, and the foundation pushed back right away. The turning point was returning to what we all already knew: people with the least access need the most help, and they have the highest rate of undiagnosed HIV. Once we were designing for them again, the answer got smaller, not bigger.</p>
		<p>So I cut the quiz down to one question.</p>

		<Pivot label="The call" heading="The fix wasn't a smarter filter. It was one question.">
			The shipped site opens with "Tap your status": positive, negative, or not sure. Answering asks a person to reflect on where they are right now. That's a small enough step to take, and taking it builds momentum toward the harder steps that follow. Each answer leads to one page written for that situation, and "not sure" leads to a test rather than a list of providers. The provider information the foundation had already collected is still there, in its own part of the site, for anyone who wants it.
		</Pivot>

		<Strip
			land
			deckLabel="Routed pages"
			figNum="Fig. 05"
			lead="Three answers, three pages, no red."
			items={[
				{
					dataLabel: "Positive",
					imgSrc: hivaz08,
					imgAlt:
						"The positive route on HIVAZ.org, teal with lime accents: 'You tested positive. Now what?' followed by 'But first, we've got to get you in care,' a category and search bar, and three links: Find care, Living with HIV, Financial assistance",
				},
				{
					dataLabel: "Negative",
					imgSrc: hivaz09,
					imgAlt:
						"The negative route, magenta with pink accents: 'You're HIV negative. Let's stay that way.' with prevention options PrEP, condoms, and PEP, and three links: Free condoms, PrEP daily prevention, PEP morning-after prevention",
				},
				{
					dataLabel: "Not sure",
					imgSrc: hivaz10,
					imgAlt:
						"The not-sure route, violet with mint accents: 'Don't know your status? Let's get you tested.' with why and how to test, a zip code search for testing locations, and three links: Understanding your results, Next steps, Free HIV home test kit",
				},
			]}
		>
			Each route has its own color and speaks to one situation. Someone who isn't sure is pointed to a test, including a free home kit, not a directory.
		</Strip>
	</section>

	<section id="outcome">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">04</span><h2>What changed</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>Measured after launch, against the site it replaced:</p>
		<Metrics
			items={[
				{ n: "−60%", label: "Bounce rate on the pages people needed to reach" },
				{ n: "−90%", label: "Smaller site structure, with the rest moved into the background" },
			]}
		/>
		<p>Program use rose too, the measure the state cared about most.</p>
		<Figure
			src={hivaz13}
			alt="The shipped site on a tablet and a phone: the tablet shows the not-sure route, 'Don't know your status? Let's get you tested', and the phone shows its Next Steps page, both in the same violet and mint"
			figNum="Fig. 06"
			lead="Built for the phone most people arrived on."
			bare
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Any screen"
		>
			The not-sure route on a tablet, and its next-steps page on a phone.
		</Figure>
	</section>

	<section id="reflection">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">05</span><h2>Looking back</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>The audience call held up. The timing didn't. The answer was inside the quiz all along, and testing a single question from the start would have found it sooner.</p>
	</section>

	<NextCaseStudy slot="close" href="/case-studies/ibm-data-lineage" label="Drawing less of the map for the questions banks ask every day" />
</CaseStudyLayout>
